\newlabel{sec:ch5_framework}{{5.2}{60}{Inverse design framework}{section.5.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Designing nanoscale light propagation by combining deep learning and twistoptics. a, Schematic representation of the inverse design workflow using a NN in twistoptics. The desired polaritonic propagation in real space is transformed into an IFC (using a fast Fourier transform, FFT) that serves as input1 to the NN (along with an additional input, input2, in which optional parameters can be specified), which predicts the optimal multilayer structure. b, The NN (dashed square) operates as a unified model that can be conceptually divided into two parts: (1) a first part where two types of input (input1 defined by $\vec  {k}$ and treated by convolutional layers, blue squares and input2 defined by $\vec  {p}$ and treated by dense layers, blue circles) generate a vector $\vec  {y}_{0}$ with the length of the parameter space, and (2) a second part consisting of a set of $M$ branches that allow finding multivalued solutions. The overall NN, trained with the customized loss function as described in [REFs], accurately predicts the structure parameters ($\theta $, $t$ and $\omega $) for one or multiple configurations.\relax }}{61}{figure.caption.27}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ch5_FIG_1}{{5.1}{61}{Designing nanoscale light propagation by combining deep learning and twistoptics. a, Schematic representation of the inverse design workflow using a NN in twistoptics. The desired polaritonic propagation in real space is transformed into an IFC (using a fast Fourier transform, FFT) that serves as input1 to the NN (along with an additional input, input2, in which optional parameters can be specified), which predicts the optimal multilayer structure. b, The NN (dashed square) operates as a unified model that can be conceptually divided into two parts: (1) a first part where two types of input (input1 defined by $\vec {k}$ and treated by convolutional layers, blue squares and input2 defined by $\vec {p}$ and treated by dense layers, blue circles) generate a vector $\vec {y}_{0}$ with the length of the parameter space, and (2) a second part consisting of a set of $M$ branches that allow finding multivalued solutions. The overall NN, trained with the customized loss function as described in [REFs], accurately predicts the structure parameters ($\theta $, $t$ and $\omega $) for one or multiple configurations.\relax }{figure.caption.27}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces NN predictions of PhP canalization and bicanalization in twisted $\alpha \text  {-}\mathrm  {MoO}_3$ bilayers. a, Schematic of the $\alpha \text  {-}\mathrm  {MoO}_3$ bilayer indicating the tunable parameters, [$t_{1}$, $t_{2}$], $\theta _1$. $\theta _1$ is the twist angle between the top and bottom layers taking the [001] crystal direction of the latter as reference system. All angles are defined as positive in the counterclockwise direction. b, Learning curve displaying the validation loss. Arrow indicates removal of the less successful branches as epochs increase. c, Predictions of the NN for 2 desired canalization propagations (parallel blue curves) at a selected frequency of $660\,\mathrm  {cm}^{-1}$. The resulting analytical IFCs (green lines) are obtained using the parameters supplied by the NN (green numbers). d, IFCs obtained numerically (TMM, displaying the imaginary part of Fresnel reflection coefficient, $\operatorname  {Im}(r_p)$) and their real-space simulations in the insets using the parameters supplied by the NN, corroborating the analytical results (overlaid green curves). e, Desired IFCs (blue curves) with non-physical solution in bilayer $\alpha \text  {-}\mathrm  {MoO}_3$ homostructures and their real-space illustrations as insets (unmatched predictions are in red).\relax }}{63}{figure.caption.28}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ch5_FIG_2}{{5.2}{63}{NN predictions of PhP canalization and bicanalization in twisted $\alpha \text {-}\mathrm {MoO}_3$ bilayers. a, Schematic of the $\alpha \text {-}\mathrm {MoO}_3$ bilayer indicating the tunable parameters, [$t_{1}$, $t_{2}$], $\theta _1$. $\theta _1$ is the twist angle between the top and bottom layers taking the [001] crystal direction of the latter as reference system. All angles are defined as positive in the counterclockwise direction. b, Learning curve displaying the validation loss. Arrow indicates removal of the less successful branches as epochs increase. c, Predictions of the NN for 2 desired canalization propagations (parallel blue curves) at a selected frequency of $660\,\mathrm {cm}^{-1}$. The resulting analytical IFCs (green lines) are obtained using the parameters supplied by the NN (green numbers). d, IFCs obtained numerically (TMM, displaying the imaginary part of Fresnel reflection coefficient, $\operatorname {Im}(r_p)$) and their real-space simulations in the insets using the parameters supplied by the NN, corroborating the analytical results (overlaid green curves). e, Desired IFCs (blue curves) with non-physical solution in bilayer $\alpha \text {-}\mathrm {MoO}_3$ homostructures and their real-space illustrations as insets (unmatched predictions are in red).\relax }{figure.caption.28}{}}
