\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces NN predictions of PhP canalization, bicanalization and tricanalization in twisted $\alpha \text  {-}\mathrm  {MoO}_3$ trilayers. a, Schematic of the trilayer homostructure indicating the tunable parameters ([$t_{1}$, $t_{2}$, $t_{3}$], [$\theta _1$, $\theta _2$]), setting the frequency as a fixed parameter input2. $\theta _1$ and $\theta _2$ are the twist angles of the middle and top layers with respect to the [001] crystal direction of the bottom layer, respectively. All angles are defined as positive in the counterclockwise direction. The thicknesses of the top, middle and bottom layers are denoted by $t_{1}$, $t_{2}$ and $t_{3}$, respectively. b, Learning curve displaying the validation loss value. The arrow indicates removal of less successful branches as epochs increase, observing an increase in the loss function as the number of branches decreases. c, Dependence of the loss jump ($\Delta L$) on the number of branches. A rapid increase can be observed starting from the removal of branch 6, confirming the need of multiple outputs. d, Left, Predictions (pred.) of the NN for two desired canalization propagations (parallel blue curves). The resulting analytical IFCs (light blue, orange and green curves) are obtained using three of the best multivalued output parameters (h). These IFCs are corroborated by numerical calculations (colour plots in the middle and right panels showing the two cases with the most different output parameters). e--g, Same as d for propagations that are forbidden when considering bilayers, such as canalization along the $\alpha \text  {-}\mathrm  {MoO}_3$ [100] crystal direction (e), bicanalization (f) and tricanalization (g). Vectors $\vec  {S}_{1}$, $\vec  {S}_{2}$ and $\vec  {S}_{3}$ in g indicate the three different propagation directions in both the numerical IFC (middle panel) and real-space numerical simulation (right panel). The results in f,g were obtained using a different NN model from a--e with nine branches, leaving the incident frequency unconstrained. h, NN output parameters for the predictions in d,e ([$t_{1}$, $t_{2}$, $t_{3}$] and [$\theta _1$, $\theta _2$]) and f,g [$t_{1}$, $t_{2}$, $t_{3}$], [$\theta _1$, $\theta _2$] and [$\omega $]).\relax }}{66}{figure.caption.29}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ch5_FIG_3}{{5.3}{66}{NN predictions of PhP canalization, bicanalization and tricanalization in twisted $\alpha \text {-}\mathrm {MoO}_3$ trilayers. a, Schematic of the trilayer homostructure indicating the tunable parameters ([$t_{1}$, $t_{2}$, $t_{3}$], [$\theta _1$, $\theta _2$]), setting the frequency as a fixed parameter input2. $\theta _1$ and $\theta _2$ are the twist angles of the middle and top layers with respect to the [001] crystal direction of the bottom layer, respectively. All angles are defined as positive in the counterclockwise direction. The thicknesses of the top, middle and bottom layers are denoted by $t_{1}$, $t_{2}$ and $t_{3}$, respectively. b, Learning curve displaying the validation loss value. The arrow indicates removal of less successful branches as epochs increase, observing an increase in the loss function as the number of branches decreases. c, Dependence of the loss jump ($\Delta L$) on the number of branches. A rapid increase can be observed starting from the removal of branch 6, confirming the need of multiple outputs. d, Left, Predictions (pred.) of the NN for two desired canalization propagations (parallel blue curves). The resulting analytical IFCs (light blue, orange and green curves) are obtained using three of the best multivalued output parameters (h). These IFCs are corroborated by numerical calculations (colour plots in the middle and right panels showing the two cases with the most different output parameters). e--g, Same as d for propagations that are forbidden when considering bilayers, such as canalization along the $\alpha \text {-}\mathrm {MoO}_3$ [100] crystal direction (e), bicanalization (f) and tricanalization (g). Vectors $\vec {S}_{1}$, $\vec {S}_{2}$ and $\vec {S}_{3}$ in g indicate the three different propagation directions in both the numerical IFC (middle panel) and real-space numerical simulation (right panel). The results in f,g were obtained using a different NN model from a--e with nine branches, leaving the incident frequency unconstrained. h, NN output parameters for the predictions in d,e ([$t_{1}$, $t_{2}$, $t_{3}$] and [$\theta _1$, $\theta _2$]) and f,g [$t_{1}$, $t_{2}$, $t_{3}$], [$\theta _1$, $\theta _2$] and [$\omega $]).\relax }{figure.caption.29}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {5.5}Experimental validation of neural-network-designed propagation}{67}{section.5.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:ch5_experiment}{{5.5}{67}{Experimental validation of neural-network-designed propagation}{section.5.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces Real-space nano-imaging of NN-designed PhP propagation in twisted multilayers. a, Top, optical image of the pre-existing twisted bilayer ($t_{1}$ = 291 nm, $t_{2}$ = 208 nm with $\theta _1$ = $130^\circ $) and a schematic of the experimental s-SNOM visualization of PhPs at the surface of a twisted $\alpha \text  {-}\mathrm  {MoO}_3$ bilayer. The contour of the top and bottom layers is marked in the optical image by black and white dashed rectangles, respectively. Bottom, near-field amplitude of the twisted bilayer, showing hyperbolic propagation of PhPs at an illuminating frequency of $675\,\mathrm  {cm}^{-1}$. The inset shows the numerically simulated propagation ($\operatorname  {Re}(E_z)$). The white reference axes indicate the optical (crystallographic) directions of the bottom layer. b, Top, schematic of the NN application, fed with the parameters of the bilayer presented in a, for achieving canalization in a twisted trilayer. Bottom, numerically calculated IFCs of the twisted trilayer for the parameters predicted by the NN. Two different desired canalizations (blue lines) are obtained for the following parameters of the third layer: 140 nm in thickness and $96^\circ $ of twist angle (pred. 1) and 280 nm in thickness and $45^\circ $ of twist angle (pred. 2). The IFC of the bilayer is also shown for comparison (yellow lines). c, Same as in a for the predicted trilayer in b (pred. 1). The added third layer is marked in the optical image with a dashed red rectangle. Bottom, near-field amplitude image showing canalization of PhPs along $\varphi _c$ = $158^\circ $ (white dashed lines) in good agreement with the NN prediction and corroborated by numerical simulations (inset).\relax }}{68}{figure.caption.30}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ch5_FIG_4}{{5.4}{68}{Real-space nano-imaging of NN-designed PhP propagation in twisted multilayers. a, Top, optical image of the pre-existing twisted bilayer ($t_{1}$ = 291 nm, $t_{2}$ = 208 nm with $\theta _1$ = $130^\circ $) and a schematic of the experimental s-SNOM visualization of PhPs at the surface of a twisted $\alpha \text {-}\mathrm {MoO}_3$ bilayer. The contour of the top and bottom layers is marked in the optical image by black and white dashed rectangles, respectively. Bottom, near-field amplitude of the twisted bilayer, showing hyperbolic propagation of PhPs at an illuminating frequency of $675\,\mathrm {cm}^{-1}$. The inset shows the numerically simulated propagation ($\operatorname {Re}(E_z)$). The white reference axes indicate the optical (crystallographic) directions of the bottom layer. b, Top, schematic of the NN application, fed with the parameters of the bilayer presented in a, for achieving canalization in a twisted trilayer. Bottom, numerically calculated IFCs of the twisted trilayer for the parameters predicted by the NN. Two different desired canalizations (blue lines) are obtained for the following parameters of the third layer: 140 nm in thickness and $96^\circ $ of twist angle (pred. 1) and 280 nm in thickness and $45^\circ $ of twist angle (pred. 2). The IFC of the bilayer is also shown for comparison (yellow lines). c, Same as in a for the predicted trilayer in b (pred. 1). The added third layer is marked in the optical image with a dashed red rectangle. Bottom, near-field amplitude image showing canalization of PhPs along $\varphi _c$ = $158^\circ $ (white dashed lines) in good agreement with the NN prediction and corroborated by numerical simulations (inset).\relax }{figure.caption.30}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.5}{\ignorespaces NN predictions of canalized polaritons from visible--near-infrared to terahertz frequencies in twisted multilayers. The top colour bar illustrates the spectral range, from the visible (violet to orange) to the near infrared (NIR) to far infrared (red gradient) and into the terahertz regime (THz) (grey). Below, schematics of the multilayer structures analysed with their NN predicted parameters ([$t_{1}$,$t_{2}$], [$\theta _1$]) that enable canalization at frequencies $\lambda $ = $0.7\,\mu \mathrm  {m}$ (visible), $\lambda $ = $10.7\,\mu \mathrm  {m}$, $11\,\mu \mathrm  {m}$, $15.1\,\mu \mathrm  {m}$ and $15.4\,\mu \mathrm  {m}$ (far infrared), and $\lambda $ = $32.3\,\mu \mathrm  {m}$ (terahertz), respectively. The corresponding analytical IFC (green curve) and the desired canalization propagations (parallel blue curves) are shown in the middle row. Real-space numerically simulated propagation ($\operatorname  {Re}(E_z)$) are displayed in the bottom row.\relax }}{70}{figure.caption.31}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ch5_FIG_5}{{5.5}{70}{NN predictions of canalized polaritons from visible--near-infrared to terahertz frequencies in twisted multilayers. The top colour bar illustrates the spectral range, from the visible (violet to orange) to the near infrared (NIR) to far infrared (red gradient) and into the terahertz regime (THz) (grey). Below, schematics of the multilayer structures analysed with their NN predicted parameters ([$t_{1}$,$t_{2}$], [$\theta _1$]) that enable canalization at frequencies $\lambda $ = $0.7\,\mu \mathrm {m}$ (visible), $\lambda $ = $10.7\,\mu \mathrm {m}$, $11\,\mu \mathrm {m}$, $15.1\,\mu \mathrm {m}$ and $15.4\,\mu \mathrm {m}$ (far infrared), and $\lambda $ = $32.3\,\mu \mathrm {m}$ (terahertz), respectively. The corresponding analytical IFC (green curve) and the desired canalization propagations (parallel blue curves) are shown in the middle row. Real-space numerically simulated propagation ($\operatorname {Re}(E_z)$) are displayed in the bottom row.\relax }{figure.caption.31}{}}
